WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.077 --> 00:00:04.660
ROGER MARTINEZ: You might have
heard of the principle of least

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:05.628
privilege--

00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:07.920
everyone should only be
granted the minimum permissions

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.300
that are needed to
perform their jobs.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.810
Grant too much access and
you've created a security risk.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.210
Grant too little access
and, soon enough, you'll

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:17.800
be inundated with
support tickets

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.600
from users requesting
that you grant

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.730
them access to do their jobs.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.310
It's a balancing act between
security and developer

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.380
productivity, and it's
one of those things that's

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.360
easier said than done.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.430
On Google Cloud,
there is a boatload

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.640
of IAM roles loaded with dozens
of individual permissions

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.380
for a wide array of
APIs and services.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.600
Google Cloud Active Assist
has a whole category

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.220
of tools under the
security umbrella

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.380
called Policy Intelligence.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.570
Policy Intelligence
uses data and machine

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.120
learning to help you
optimize your IAM policies,

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.257
troubleshoot access
issues, better predict

00:00:51.257 --> 00:00:53.590
the impact of any permission
changes across your project

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.072
or organization, and
simplify access reviews.

00:00:57.072 --> 00:00:59.530
Let's do a quick walkthrough
of the services and tools that

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.860
make up Policy Intelligence
by imagining a few scenarios

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.132
and asking a few questions
that might come up.

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:08.340
Let's say that you've got
a Cloud storage bucket that

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.470
has some sensitive data in it.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.900
Question-- who in
my organization

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.240
can read the files
in this bucket?

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.000
Answer that with Policy
Analyzer, which can tell you

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.760
which user accounts,
service accounts, and groups

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.200
have certain roles
and permissions

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.090
on a specific
resource, including

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.310
access gained through service
account impersonation.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.948
OK, how about this?

00:01:28.948 --> 00:01:31.240
You've got a new employee
joining the team who actually

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.260
does need to use the
files in that bucket,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.200
but it looks like
they can't access them

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.910
with their user account.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.420
The question-- why can't
they access the bucket?

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.870
The answer to this one is found
with Policy Troubleshooter.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.930
You give it an email, a
resource, and a permission,

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.750
and Policy Troubleshooter will
examine the IAM policies that

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.930
apply to the resource to reveal
whether any of the user's roles

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.240
include the required
permissions.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.740
It will also tell
you whether or not

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.250
the user is in a group
that has those permissions.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.088
Next, you're doing an audit
of your project's IAM roles

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:04.630
and permissions and
find that there's

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.400
a service account
that's been used in one

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.229
of your application's backends.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:11.080
First, how do you make sure that
this service account is still

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.160
needed?

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.100
For that, there's
Activity Analyzer.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.770
Activity Analyzer lets you
see when your service accounts

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.550
and keys were last used
to call a Google API.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.100
With the dates of the most
recent authentication activity,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.410
you can more easily
identify service accounts

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.170
and service account keys
that may no longer be in use

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.200
and safe to
deactivate or delete.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.740
OK, what if you learn that, yes,
the service account is still

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.440
in use, but it's
also overprovisioned

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.470
with extra permissions
you think it doesn't need?

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.870
What would happen if you
swapped out one of those roles

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.790
for a more restricted one?

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:46.870
Answer?

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.390
Well, you could just make the
change and see what breaks.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:54.100
But that's probably not
the best course of action.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.070
Instead, you can use Policy
Simulator to simulate a policy

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.020
change to make sure
nothing breaks,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.960
and apply the change if you're
satisfied with the results.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.410
This removes the risk
of accidentally breaking

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.240
an application or
process by removing

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.380
a role that you incorrectly
thought was unnecessary.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.090
Finally, when it comes to the
principle of least privilege,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:16.420
there's one more question
that you might have.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:18.730
How do I start and make
progress when I have

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.070
lots of overprovisioned roles?

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.960
Well, here's a great answer--

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.850
IAM Recommender.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.280
These are recommendations
that automatically surface

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.960
on your Cloud
Console, letting you

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.850
know when accounts
may be overprovisioned

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:32.720
with too many permissions.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.530
These recommendations are
based on actual usage data

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:38.260
and include alternative roles
that you can grant instead

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.600
with just a couple of clicks.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.790
Recommendations are
updated constantly,

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.040
and you have the option of
dismissing them or applying

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:45.540
them.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.540
And, even after that, you can
easily roll back the changes.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.540
Together, these tools make
up Active Assist Policy

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.880
Intelligence that makes
applying the principle of least

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.340
privilege less of a burden.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.165
In the next couple of
videos in the series,

00:03:59.165 --> 00:04:01.540
I'm going to dig a little
deeper into each of these tools

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.090
so that you can get familiar
with what they're capable of

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.698
and understand when you
would use each of them.

00:04:07.698 --> 00:04:09.990
In the meantime, if you want
to learn more about Policy

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.600
Intelligence, check out
cloud.google.com/policy

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.050
intelligence.

